<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Loyalty Program Management Loyalty Program Management AF includes functions that define and oversee the creation of, operation of, and evolution of customer loyalty programs.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Information Support -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Information Support">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.4.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">90</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Information Support Customer Information Support Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of collecting, qualifying and managing Customer information and also personalizing Customer profile.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Collect and Qualify Customer Information -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Collect and Qualify Customer Information">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.4.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">91</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Collect and Qualify Customer Information The Collect and Qualify Customer Information Aggregate Function concerns the management of our knowledge of the individual customer to keep or produce an up-to-date, accurate and legally compliant Customer information. 

It will incorporate into the customer profile, all relevant information gathered through all contacts with the customer (demographics, life stage, household, community of interest, business direction) and customer experience offered (quality of service experienced, customer inquiries).
Customer and market information from other sources may be gathered, which will build a better understanding of the customer.

Customer Information must be made available to any process that needs to access it. 
This customer information will be used to continually refine the means and style of interaction, and the solution sets.

Knowledge and consent of the party may be required to store any collected information and to use it (example: opt-in).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Information Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Information Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.4.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">92</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Information Management The Customer Information Management Aggregate Function enables the creation and updates of customer information such as name, address(es) and site(s) location, organisation, contact person and contact person roles (legal representative, subscriber, payer, end-user, fleet manager) and contact methods.
The term Customer is used in the broad sense: it represents here person or organisation, potential, existing or former customer.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Personalize Customer Interaction -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Personalize Customer Interaction">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.4.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">93</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Personalize Customer Interaction The Personalize Customer Interaction Aggregate Function concerns the definition of scripts to make commercial dialog easier and more efficient between customers (including users or prospects) and Customer Service Representative (CSR) or Interactive Voice Response (IVR), 
These scripts can be used during any type of interaction, either to make qualification or sales as well as polls or assistance.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Behavior Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Behavior Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.4.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">94</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Behavior Management Customer Behavior Management Aggregate Function all information related to the customer behavior and actions to be triggered accordingly.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Behavior Tracking -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Behavior Tracking">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.4.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">95</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Behavior Tracking The Customer Behavior Tracking function concerns the necessary functionality to monitor the Customer Behavior during its relationships with the CSP, public communications and its products uses.
It includes:

	- Track of the contacts by email, SMS, phone, mail and Web (Web use, chat) to provide a detailed and precise understanding of the customer behavior in front of the Telco operator solicitations (marketing campaigns, customer inquiries) or during a customer request.
	- Track all user steps on the web site and influence on social networks: click-stream, viewed pages, consulted product descriptions, comments. In real time, this function analyses the user behavior, identifies his centers of interest and then deduces his profile which will be used to push contents and offers adequate for the user / customer needs.
	- Track of the customer behavior in order to provide a global and personalized vision of its real consumption on all its products based on a detailed view of each product consumption.
</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.5.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">96</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Order Management Customer Order Management functions manage the end to end lifecycle of a Customer Order request from the establishment to the complete delivery. This includes Customer Order Establishment, Customer Order Tracking and LifeCycle Management, as well as Customer Order Orchestration.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Repository Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Repository Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.5.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">97</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Order Repository Management Customer Order Repository Management Aggregate Function tracks and manages the events and the lifecycle related to the Customer Order and to its items (e.g. : Order lines).
When the Customer Order is completed, this function publishes the event Order Completed. This event usually triggers the Customer Order Orchestration function to deliver the Customer Order.
It gathers product items delivery events from Customer Order Orchestration and manages related Customer Order lifecycle. It also manages these statuses in case of Order cancellation.
Depending on the Customer Order (or any of its elements) events, and on the implemented business rules, this function can decide to notify automatically the customer of the Customer Order improvement. The notification itself can be made by SMS / email / other medium and is done by the functions of the Welcome and Interaction Aggregate Function.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Offer and Product Configuration -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Offer and Product Configuration">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.5.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">98</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Offer and Product Configuration The Offer and Product Configuration function enables the configuration of the commercial offer chosen by the customer (period of engagement for example) and the products it markets (for examples: telephone number, quantity of SMS/MMS, hours of voice calls, ...). 
The configuration covers the choice of optional offers and products, the choice of the functions values, etc.
It can be based on product configurator using a rule engine.
It can be used at the same time by Selling, Order Establishment or Develop Sales Proposal Business activities.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Eligibility Validation -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Eligibility Validation">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.5.1.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">99</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Order Eligibility Validation The Customer Order Commercial Eligibility Aggregate Function validates that the Offer &amp; products specified on the Customer Order, are available and feasible from a commercial and functional point of view.
It includes:
- Commercial Eligibility with commercial compatibility with the already customer installed Offers and with customer profile (age, location, ) 
- Functional Eligibility with functional compatibility with the already customer installed Products.
- Customer Credit Eligibility check, considering the payment history and the credit scores for a Customer, only the predictive credit score for a Prospective Customer.
The technical feasibility at the product level is verified relying on the Service Availability function.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Quotation -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Quotation">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.5.1.5</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">100</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Order Quotation The Customer Order Quotation Aggregate Function enables the elaboration of an estimate, including configuration, pricing and feasibility elements, thanks to the specific configuration, eligibility and valuation functions, identifying the various relevant interlocutors (through the Sales Interlocutors Management function), the scenarios to be realized and the lot.
The quotation can be materialized via a document and/or associated to a simplified identification mean (QR code, bar code, ) to ease later interactions and identification of the quotation, and sent to the customer.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Completion -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Completion">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.5.1.6</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">101</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Order Completion The Customer Order Completion function enables to complete and finalize the Customer Order, after its validation with the requester and before triggering its delivery, with collect of Customer Data.
The complements could concern customer order links with actors, dates, appointment, address, billing account.

The final validation of the Customer Order can trigger the generation of documents such as:
- a contract document, summing up the customer order, and the standard and specific sales terms
- a simplified identification mean such as QR / bar code, and its sending to the customer, by MMS, email,  to ease later interactions and identification of the order. Example: to pick up in store a goods product ordered from web portal or mobile apps, the customer just needs to present the sent QR / bar code.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Change Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Change Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.5.1.7</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">102</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Order Change Management Customer Order Change Management Aggregate Function amends pending order resulted from customer change requests or provisioning system limitation and revalidate the order.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Validation -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Validation">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.5.1.8</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">103</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Order Validation The Customer Order Validation Aggregate Function permits to validate the Customer Order, or the cancellation of a Customer Order, with the requester, according to Offer and Product catalogue and installed base (see Customer Order Eligibility function), and to provisioning rules and delays, after tariff calculation with possibly Framework agreement special conditions (see Tariff calculation and rating function).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Orchestration -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Orchestration">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.5.1.9</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">104</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Order Orchestration The Customer Order Orchestration Aggregate Function provides workflow and orchestration capabilities at the Product Order Item level to deliver a dedicated Customer Order. 

Orchestration can take into account :
- Constraints between Product Order Items inside the Customer Order, or successive Customer Orders including modification or cancellation (in-flight changes)
- Any type of business rules based on information even external to the Customer Order. For example : high level of priority for VIP customers
- Triggering of exception process or delivery planning update, depending on Customer Order or Service Order events.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Delivery Preparation -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Order Delivery Preparation">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.5.1.10</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">105</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Order Delivery Preparation The Customer Order Delivery Preparation Aggregate Function allows in the context of a customer order to:
- query catalogues and installed bases to identify what needs to be delivered: Service Specification (CFS SPec) and its configuration, Stock Item, Supplier Product, Work Spec, related to the ordered product,
- prepare a Service Order (CFS Order), Supplier Order, Stock Item Order or Work Order with the necessary information.
In the case of a Product associated with an Internal Service (Know-How), this function also allows to: 
- check if a corresponding Installed CFS is operational in the Service Installed Base, and so determine the operation at CFS level (creation or modification)
- possibly group in the same Service Order several ordered products, based on the same CFS specification, and/or identified to be delivered at the same time by the Customer Order Delivery Orchestration.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Voucher Activation Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Voucher Activation Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.5.1.11</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">106</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Voucher Activation Management The (material or electronic) voucher materializes for example a mobile top-up, a refund, a promotional code. 
The Voucher Activation Aggregate Function performs voucher activation management, after it was bought or ordered and paid.
It checks validity and authenticity of voucher, and marks it as used once it has been completed.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Problem Support -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Problem Support">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.6.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">107</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Problem Support Customer Problem Support Aggregate Function contains functions responsible for the management of customer request concerning problem, assistance or information.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Assistance -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Assistance">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.6.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">108</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Assistance The Customer Assistance Aggregate Function permits to bring a help to qualify a dysfunction indicated by a user or customer and to suggest basic solution to restore a properly functioning. 
This can be built from list of questions (script), previous experiences, knowledge bases and best-practice descriptions, chat with a CSR,  according to the dysfunction typology. 
It may comprise:
- identification of the customer - or user - and verifying authorization to access assistance via Person Identification and Authentication function
- information on how to use a product, best practices
- check actions like reboot (to see if the dysfunction continues), check of the subscription, configuration check, 
- identification of the product(s) type(s) concerned by a dysfunction
- information concerning collective trouble which may impact the customer or user
   When further to a collective trouble, a message of information is identified by the Service Impact Assessment function, the Customer Assistance function is in charge of implementing this communication (on various support like chat, web, Interactive Voice Server).

If during this phase, the dysfunction is confirmed, the Assurance Process will continue with the Product Trouble Ticket Management and/or Product Test function.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Case Workflow Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Case Workflow Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.6.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">109</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Case Workflow Management The Customer Case Workflow Management Aggregate Function is in charge of identifying steps of the case, sequencing them and orchestrating the Case Workflow.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Financial Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Financial Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.7.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">110</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Financial Management Customer Financial Management Aggregate Function groups functions which manage the processing of financial transactions affecting the customer's financial account.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Bill Dispute -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Bill Dispute">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.7.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">111</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Bill Dispute Bill Dispute Aggregate Function consists of handling the customer inquiries received through customer service, self-care or any other channel, and for all type of product, about their bills that have not been solved by simple actions in the Welcome process (e.g. walk customer through their invoice).
- The initial customer contact and the disputed amount are recorded.
- The case is investigated using billing data (invoice images, billed items, usage history) A/R data (e.g. search of unmatched payment) and others (e.g. installed products, network databases).
- The dispute settlement results in customer notification of the results, dispute granted (for the whole disputed amount or part of it) or customer request denied.
- The case is closed.
When the dispute is granted, the granted amount must be taken off the debt, through a debit adjustment. Depending on business rules in force, this may be billed either as part of the following periodic invoice, or as a specific invoicing process and related accounting document (in this case a credit note).
The reason for the adjustment must be recorded, for example adjustment may be a gesture of goodwill from the company, or a correction for a proven billing error. Depending on local law and regulation, the process and applicable rules may be different.
In addition, if the investigation showed an error in product and services data (e.g. wrong service affected to a contract), the settlement will also include an update request to the installed offers and products repository.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Payment Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Payment Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.7.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">112</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Payment Management The Payment Management function is aimed at processing all payment flows issued or received by the Enterprise:
- Regardless of the payment method (address all payment methods supported by the Enterprise), for example a cash payment or a payment with loyalty points to buy an accessory, a payment on invoice to pay the subscription, a payment via a scheduled plan to buy a device
- For prepaid as well as post-paid offers.
- Immediate payments are also recorded (for instance for offer including equipment or terminals sales)
- it also includes management of deposits related to orders.

It comprises the main following sub-functions.
Payment flow management which role is to:
- Manage all of the relations with the banking sector (unique portal), both outgoing (direct debit requests, payments by credit card, refunds by cheque or by bank transfer, etc.) and incoming (for example for bank transfers and cheques files, payment rejections, etc.) for customers,
- Manage all flows related to banking (supervision, management of bank opening schedules) and allow a rapid response in the case of problems.
It includes:
- Formatting of the orders: to the 3rd party systems for outgoing payments (depending on the Electronic Funds Transfer (EFT) agreements with banks or other 3rd parties: e.g. Electronic Data Interchange (EDI) message) and to the originating systems for the incoming payments,
- Grouping and ordering of orders (e. g. group payment requests by target company and source company).

It handles both outgoing (mostly payment requests, occasional refunds), and incoming events (payments or rejections).
- Payment management
This functionality handles the business logic needed to perform a payment request that can require on-line interface with the banking system. It addresses use cases such as :
1. Payment request to top-up prepaid balance / account
A payer wishes to pay on-line for a prepaid account top-up (payer may be the account holder or not),
2. Direct Payment related to a customer order (on-line or in shops) : sale of a device, or deposit for a rented device
3. Payment request to settle a debt.
A payer wishes to pay for one invoice (main case), or a subset of an invoice (partial payment).
- Payment tractability and banking reconciliation
Generically, the payment functionality should involve tracing and querying capabilities that allow to trace back incoming payments to the originating event (outgoing payment request or invoice). A payment can be partial.
- Payment recycling / re-allocation
Depending on the implementation of interfaces with banking or other 3rd party systems, it may happen that incoming payments are received with ambiguous data, i.e. cannot be traced back with certainty to an originating event. In this case, a recycling functionality is needed.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Dunning -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Dunning">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.7.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">113</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Dunning The Dunning Aggregate Function allows the different steps of the dunning to be managed until its resolution (payment or write off).
It covers soft and hard dunning phases (soft dunning ends with contract termination).
It comprises the following sub-functions:
- Update financial scoring: update of the customer scoring information, based on events that are significant of payer behavior of the customer (e.g. late payment, delays, etc.) 
- Initiate dunning strategy: implementation of the companys dunning strategy within its information system. 
Example: A payment schedule is assigned according to a variety of customer criteria, such as customer type, payment means, payment behavior, etc.
- Manage the dunning and event process: Management of the dunning process pertaining to a dossier and the automatic or manual chaining of actions, as issuing a SMS, a letter, an outbound call, a barring action (suspension, restriction or even contract termination, via an order) or delay fees. 
- Business and follow-up activities: Control and management of the dunning activity. It generates statistics.
- Penalty Charges: Automatic and manual generation of the penalty charges (dunning charges, payment rejection fees) linked to dunning of the due amount, in order to bill the customer according to the details of his / her contract..
- Payment delay agreement and promises: Inclusion of a payment schedule or a promise of payment, negotiated with the customer during the dunning process. 
- Write off: When the debt is considered as irrecoverable, the amount of the debt is written off (the dunning file is closed and the customer subledger accounting is informed).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Loan Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Loan Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.7.1.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">114</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Loan Management Loan Management Aggregate Function includes functions involved in managing the lifecycle and administration of Loans, as viewed from the perspective of the lender.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Invoice Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Invoice Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.7.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">115</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Invoice Management The Invoice Management Aggregated Function groups functions related to the production of the customer invoices and to prevention activities.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Billing Account Administration -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Billing Account Administration">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.7.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">116</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Billing Account Administration The Billing Account administration Aggregate Function manages all the information related to the billing account, needed to produce an invoice and agreed with the customer such as :
- The associated invoicing group (linked to the periodicity and the date of the invoice production),
- Perimeter of installed contracts and products charged to the billing account, and for which any type of charges can be collected (one time charges, recurring charges or usage charges),
- Parties and party roles concerned by the billing process, such as Holder, Payer, Bill receiver, etc.,
- Invoice structure and presentation rules,
- And possibly default payment means for automatic generation of payments.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Invoicing -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Invoicing">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.7.2.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">117</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Invoicing The Invoicing Aggregate Function comprises two steps: bill composition (i.e. sorting and assembly of bill items) and bill production. 
It enables either physical or electronic invoices, through the formatting and generation of final form (paper, CD-ROM, pdf ) and delivery (sending by e-mail, post or displaying through a web self-care portal). 
On electronic format, the invoice can include tools enabling consumption analysis for corporate needs for instance.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Risk Mitigation Measures Enforcement -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Risk Mitigation Measures Enforcement">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.7.2.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">118</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Risk Mitigation Measures Enforcement The Risk Mitigation Measures Enforcement Aggregate Function enforces the Risk Mitigation  scenarios previously defined by the Customer Risk Mitigation Definition, that is:
- Detection of billable amounts threshold (according to billing account owners risk profile and related scenario),
- Triggering of actions (e.g.: sending alert SMS, triggering product barring) via dedicated functions.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Loyalty and Retention -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Loyalty and Retention">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.8.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">119</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Loyalty and Retention Customer Loyalty and Retention Aggregate Function groups functions related to the use of loyalty schemes to attract and gain the loyalty of customers, and to the retention of acquired customers.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Risk Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Risk Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.8.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">120</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Risk Management Customer Risk Management Aggregate Function is based on information collected from all internal processes and that consistent risk assessment is used across the Enterprise Detection to manage with customer axes, fraud risk, influence risk, and churn risk.
It includes also detection of the outstanding payment risks, for a new customer or during a new order for example, with:
- Definition of the class "bad debtors, from analysis of the profiles of bad debtor behaviors.
- Score calculation from collected information related to the customer</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Personalize Customer Profile -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Personalize Customer Profile">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.8.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">121</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Personalize Customer Profile The Personalize Customer Profile Aggregate Function includes:

	- Steering of the commercial pressure: manages the number of contacts with a customer in order to solicit him only if appropriate. The commercial pressure concerns the under and over customer solicitation.
	- Personalization of the customer relationship: based on a follow-up of the customer behavior, the aim is to select some key elements in order to personalize the relation during the customer contacts (contacts with the Telco operator services initiated by the customer).
	- Identification of the interaction channels by customer or segment: Identification of the optimal interaction channel for each customer or customer segment, based on the profile study and the history of the contacts.
</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Value Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Value Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.8.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">122</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Value Management The Customer Value Management Aggregate Function comprises:

	- Measure and follow up of the customer value: Development of a model in order to determine the customer value from its uses (traffic, products and services used), and corresponding revenues and costs. The follow-up of the customer value evolution (identification of the evolution tendencies of the value and actions concerning the creation or destruction of value, Average Revenue Per User, margin per user).
	- Predictive Scoring of the new customer behavior: using a predictive model based on the histories of customers having the similar profile, determine the expected evolution of the customer value and his lifetime. Customer scoring is performed when customers sign up a contract, then enriched and updated by events occurring during the customer lifecycle (e.g. revenue generated, usage patterns, payment behavior...). The output of customer scoring could be a risk level or an opportunity.
	- Segmentation based on the customer value and/or lifetime value: Regrouping of the customers in relevant sets (even subsets) for Marketing. The segmentation criteria use the customer value, the customer lifetime value, the product and service uses (consumption behaviors).
</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Welcome and Interaction -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Welcome and Interaction">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.8.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">123</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Welcome and Interaction Welcome and Interaction Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of managing all interfaces between the company and the existing or prospective customer.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Interaction Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Interaction Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.8.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">124</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Interaction Management The Customer Interaction Management Aggregate Function manages all type of interactions with Customers (e.g. customer, prospect, product user, ) and enables recording such interactions (duration, type and reason), manages interactions follow-up, escalation and historical background.

An interaction can be initiated:
- by a Customer (inbound contact), through the Welcome process, to identify his needs, problems, 
- by the CSP or one of its representative (outbound contact), through the Campaign Execution, Welcome, Order Follow up,  processes, 
     - to inform the customer about our offers, promotions, 
     - or about his on-going requests, queue position, 
     - or to alert him in case of network problem, over consumption, payment delay, </rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Context Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Context Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.8.2.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">125</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Context Management Customer Context Management Aggregate Function allows to define the needed information and the first actions to be initiated once the person has been identified and authenticated. Moreover, information and actions should be set in accordance with the channel used by the actors (human, system).
Different rules have to be set in accordance with:
- Context of the request
- Role of the person initiating the request
- Customer contract situation
- List of possible actions with priorities
- The CSR or seller role and expertise level, if present, in the context of the request
- Presentation rules for the channel or device involved.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Queue Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Queue Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.8.2.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">126</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Queue Management Queue Management Aggregate Function permits to manage queues when customers need to interact with the CSP customer service representatives (CSR), whatever the channel:

	- in shops, the customer can go to the reception desk, be identified via NFC or can self-register via automatic desk
	- by phone, via the customer phone number, the customer identifier, ...
	- on web portal, via the customer credentials


When an interaction is initialized and if the CSR can't be immediately identified or is not available, the Queue Management Aggregate Function permits to associate the interaction to the relevant team - or team member - queue, depending on the type of request.
It can also manage

	- priority assignment, depending on the customer (ex: premium customer) or on type of request
	- waiting time calculation - and customer information
	- possibility to make an appointment


When the CSR is available, this function can trigger a SMS push, for example, to inform the customer (via the Interaction Management function).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Request Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Customer Request Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">3.8.2.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">127</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Customer Request Management Customer Request Management Aggregate Functionallows:
1) The capture of all information required by the Customer Request according to its type or nature. Depending on the type or nature of the Customer Request, the list of information to capture may strongly vary. Depending also on information captured, additional information may be required. The Customer Request information capture shall use configuration parameters and rules to determine which information needs to be captured and when.
2) The qualification of the Customer Request according to configured criteria and information captured. Customer Request Management shall be able to determine the result of the captured Customer Request. This result may either be: the triggering of the Sales subdomain functions (from Offer and Product Development, Management and Operation Domain), the generation of a Customer Problem (that may correspond to a Bill Dispute), the generation of a Product Trouble Ticket.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Strategy Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Strategy Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">128</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Strategy Management Service Strategy Management Aggregate Function groups functions useful to define the service strategy and to develop it (new services or enhance the existing services).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Strategy Design and Planning -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Strategy Design and Planning">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.1.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">129</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Strategy Design and Planning The Service Strategy, Design and Planning Aggregate Function is responsible for the definition of the service strategy at the company level. This function takes into account a list of inputs to determine the need to implement new services, to modify or replace existing services or to stop them. As inputs, the function can use the following information:
- The existing context of the company with available services and resources (network, PFS)
- The data produced by the Service Usage Analysis function
- The performance, quality and test results of existing services or in test services (like POC to test new services in run)
- The new services and underlying technologies that are developed in the industry (following services trend, new generation of network, competitors...) and evaluation of the appeal for these new solutions including information provided by the Offer and Product Strategy function
- Marketing constraints concerning the date of opening for new service
- The cost of the existing and new services and technologies with benefit evaluation
- The possibility or the obligation to provide the services to third parties
- The resource strategy defined in a bottom up process (techno-push)

Following the definition of the service strategy, and after the definition of the resource strategy and the resource design, the Service Strategy, Design and Planning function updates the service design by requesting the modifications (creating, updating, deleting) of the service specifications to the service catalogue and thus the evolution of the know-how.

Finally, the Service Strategy, Design and Planning function provides a high level planning of the strategy implementation. This high level plan contains for example the choice of technology with selected options (VHBB with FTTH or RFOG or both), the site management strategy, the selection of partners to provide contents The strategy planning can take into account constraints (like legal constraints or the need of a validation by authorities) and notify other companies, local authorities or governments of the chosen strategy.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Analysis -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Analysis">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">130</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Analysis Service Strategic Analysis Aggregate Function groups functions useful to conduct delayed analysis, on the collected information concerning service, which may have an influence on the strategy.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Diagnostic and Test Relevance Quality Loop -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Diagnostic and Test Relevance Quality Loop">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.1.2.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">131</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Diagnostic and Test Relevance Quality Loop Service Diagnostic and Test Relevance Quality Loop Aggregate Function provides the necessary functionalities to manage a quality loop on diagnostic to continually tweak and improve it.
Functionalities include:

	- Management of success/failure result feedback of diagnostic and correction provided by technicians or the systems after a correction
	- Offline analysis of the success/failure data compared to the diagnostic and correction rules to provide modifications to improve them. This analysis is done using statistical diagnostic data and test results to evaluate their relevance and the needed updates.

The provided improvements can be used to update the definition of diagnostic rules and correction actions in the Service Problem Policy Management function and the definition of tests in the Service Test Policy Management function.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Usage Analysis -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Usage Analysis">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.1.2.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">132</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Usage Analysis The Service Usage Analysis Aggregate Function comprises analysis of the Service use and traffic and calculates Service Usage forecast between operators according to Service Usage Analysis.

It includes forecasts of traffic between operators in order to:
- allow traffic visualization according to various criteria (produced, country, carriers, services, routings)
- analyze the traffic measured, developed and billed with KPIs such as volume in minutes, number of communications, number of messages, amount valued / charged expressed by nature by call, by products, countries, cities or regions, by operators, by network type (2G, 3G, 4G), by contracts on the market international Wholesale in purchase / sales.
- deliver mobile KPIs such as the number of unique Roaming users per product (Month To Date / Year To Date)
- deliver analysis to optimize the network elements involved.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Capability Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Capability Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">133</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Capability Management Service Capability Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of specifying and managing Service Specification capabilities.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Resource Check -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Resource Check">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.2.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">134</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Resource Check Service Test Resource Check Aggregate Function is in charge of defining, implementing and checking resources used to test Services.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Deployment and Retirement -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Deployment and Retirement">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.2.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">135</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Deployment and Retirement The Service Deployment and Retirement Aggregate Function is responsible for implementing the service strategy high level plan and develop the service infrastructure that will be used to provide the service (through products) to the customers at large (mass market, enterprise, to third parties/partners...) The Service Deployment and Retirement function manages the deployment constraints like area limitation (for test or for marketing purposes), progressive deployment following precise milestones

The service deployment and retirement can be responsible to manage constraints (like legal constraints or the need of a validation by authorities) and notify other companies, local authorities or governments of the effective deployment or retirement of a service.

Based on the resources developed in the Resource Installed Base, the Service Installed Base is updated and pre-configured if needed, for example for generic services with no specific customer configuration.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.3.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">136</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Quality and Performance Development The Service Quality and Performance Management Aggregate Function groups functions that supervise service functioning and manage resulting alarms.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Performance Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Performance Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.3.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">137</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Performance Development Service Performance Development Aggregate Function enables to define the strategy and policy concerning supervision at service level, that is to say the indicators to calculate, to measure service performance and quality, and the conditions of alarm activation.
It includes:
- definition of indicators associated to the service types (declension of the pattern metrics at the service level), with thresholds, calculation rules and consequences
     - example: rate of not cut communications (=maintained 2 minutes), availability indicator
- definition of the alarm setting off rules 
- definition of severity and risk level evaluation rules 
     - internal rule with regard to the area of the impact
     - rule linked to the contract (SLA)
          - bringing constraints on the interruption slot or requiring to plan the interruption with the customer in the case of scheduled works.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Quality Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Quality Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.3.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">138</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Quality Development Service Quality Development Aggregate Function provides establishment of Service quality indicators specifications and the rules connected to them.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Performance Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Performance Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.3.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">139</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Performance Management Service Performance Management aggregate function groups functions that both proactively and reactively optimize the delivery of services to customers.  This can include a real-time, end-to-end view to ensure that each service is functioning correctly as well as a historical view. These functions build on the Resource Performance data and active end-end service performance test data to provide a view of a service.They provide a key input to determine the Quality of Service.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Specification Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Specification Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.3.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">140</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Specification Development The Service Specification Development Aggregate Function enable to create and update the catalog at Service level :
- definition of the Service Providers know-hows (Customer Facing Services specification) and pre-requisite
- definition of the technical solutions available for each know-how (Resource Facing Services specification)
- links with the Supplier Product Catalog for know-hows, technical solutions or part of technical solutions not realized by the Service Provider itself.
It is also responsible for implementing the service strategy high level plan and develop the service infrastructure that will be used to provide the service (through products) to the customers at large (mass market, enterprise, to third parties/partners...) The Service Deployment and Retirement function manages the deployment constraints like area limitation (for test or for marketing purposes), progressive deployment following precise milestones</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Technical Solution Policy Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Technical Solution Policy Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.3.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">141</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Technical Solution Policy Management The Technical Solution Policy Management Aggregate Function enables to define - and to check consistency of - potentially complex rules to automate the choice of the technical solution for a know-how - or parts of the technical solution.

Criteria can be of various nature and combined :
- geographical
- depending on the chosen characteristics values of the know-how
- depending on other products / know-hows of the customer - installed or ordered
- depending on the current state of Service Platforms, Network equipment (occupation rate, load balancing,  )
- depending on Supplier/Partner contracts (volume discounts, )</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Specification Design -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Specification Design">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.3.2.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">142</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Specification Design The Service Specification Design Aggregate Function enables to create and update the catalogue at Service level :
- definition of the Service Providers know-hows (CustomerFacingServices specification) and pre-requisite
- definition of the technical solutions available for each know-how (ResourceFacingServicesspecification)
- links with the Supplier Product Catalogue for know-hows, technical solutions or part of technical solutions not realized by the Service Provider itself.

These know-hows specifications (and their technical solutions specifications) can then be used to define Products specifications which will be ordered by customers and delivered, but also for the internal needs of the Service Provider to manage its Service Platforms and network infrastructure process.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Specification Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Specification Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.3.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">143</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Specification Development Service Test Specification Development Aggregate Function functions provide the necessary functionalities to manage the service test catalog containing the service test specifications, the rules that define the test configuration setting up based on the context, the rules that define the strategies for executing the test as well as how the test results should be restituted.
Test strategies can range from simple to complex and encompass the definition of test scenario.
The definition of the test (with specification and rules) is very important because it defines the question that is asked and thus shapes the result that is reported back.
The functionalities include providing:
- Service Test Specification and test scenario management
  - Test scenario management: for example, a home equipment self-test can trigger a test scenario that verifies the functioning of all the services associated, depending on the installed products of the user. Thus, the Box could test its internal configuration and functioning and its capacity to connect to internet and VoIP services.
  - Managing service test capabilities: some resources used to implement services have limited capabilities for executing tests, especially for multiple parallel testing. All the constraints linked to limited resources test capabilities and depending on the execution context must be aggregated and managed at service test level.
  - Managing initiator authorization: some tests are restricted to some user roles (internal or external) so the authorization are defined at the Service Test Strategy and Policy Management
- Test rules defining the strategies for setting up test configuration
- Test rules defining how to carrying out the service test
  - For example: to choose the next test in a scenario depending on the results of the previous tests
- Test rules defining the enrichment of test results. 
  - For example: enrichment of internet bandwidth measure based on installed product
- Test rules defining the restitution of the result depending on test execution context
   - For example: a test result of ok/not ok can be enough for a user whereas customer care requires more details in the result of the test.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Policy Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Policy Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.3.3.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">144</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Policy Management Service Test Policy Management Aggregate Function provides the necessary functionalities to manage the service test catalogue containing the service test specifications, the rules that define the test configuration setting up based on the context, the rules that define the strategies for executing the test as well as how the test results should be restituted.
Test execution strategies can range from simple to complex and encompass the definition of test scenario.
The definition of the test (with specification and rules) is very important because it defines the question that is asked and thus shapes the result that is reported back.
The functionalities include providing:
- Service Test Specification and test scenario management
  - Test scenario management: for example, a home equipment self-test can trigger a test scenario that verifies the functioning of all the services associated, depending on the installed products of the user. Thus, the box could test its internal configuration and functioning and its capacity to connect to internet and VoIP services.
  - Managing service test capabilities: some resources used to implement services have limited capabilities for executing tests, especially for multiple parallel testing. All the constraints linked to limited resources test capabilities and depending on the execution context must be aggregated and managed at service test level.
  - Managing initiator authorization: some tests are restricted to some user roles (internal or external) so the authorization are defined at the Service Test Strategy and Policy Management
- Test rules defining the strategies for setting up test configuration
- Test rules defining how to carrying out the service test
  - For example: to choose the next test in a scenario depending on the results of the previous tests
- Test rules defining the enrichment of test results. 
  - For example: enrichment of internet bandwidth measure based on installed product
- Test rules defining the restitution of the result depending on test execution context
   - For example: a test result of ok/not ok can be enough for a user whereas customer care requires more details in the result of the test.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.4.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">145</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Management Service Management groups functions in charge of managing the inventory of installed services.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Repository Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Repository Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.4.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">146</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Repository Management The Service Repository Management Aggregate Function stores and maintains the instances of know-hows (CustomerFacingServices) corresponding to products, ordered by customers, their configuration and links between them (ex: relies on).

It also describes and maintains, for each installed know-how (or service), the links with the chosen technical solution (ResourceFacingServiceSpec and corresponding Resources) which permit to manage the change of a technical solution - or part of it - for instances of know-how (example: new generation of Service Platform to deploy).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Impact Assessment -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Impact Assessment">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.4.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">147</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Impact Assessment The Impact Assessment Aggregate Function enables the calculation of the impact of a resource event or set of events, depending on severity and risk level evaluation rules defined by the Service Performance Developement function.

The resource event can be: 
- a resource trouble or a resource trouble ticket
- an alarm on the resource
- a work planned on the resource (ResourceOrder issued from a Delivery process, from infrastructure operations, from problem management ) or a Project concerning the resource
The nature and the estimated duration of the event can be used for the impact analysis. 

Different types of evaluation rules can be defined, for example:
- rules based on the volume of the impact (example: number of installed services using an installed resource)
- rules based on the related service level agreement (example : a unique installed service can be impacted but with a very high SLA) 

This function provides functionality to:
- identify impacted elements: makes the link between the impacted installed resource and directly or indirectly impacted installed services and installed products (example: an access trouble impacts services supported by the access)
- determine the level, severity and the risk level of the impact,
- helps to plan the execution in appropriate time slot, when customer agreement is required and/or to comply with SLA
- make sure that the means to process the impact are correctly identified, affected and overseen.
- get back the necessary information to be able to notify the concerned customers.

In an assurance context, this function consumes information such as Installed Resource and Service (including SLA), ResourceTrouble, ResourceTroubleTicket, ResourceAlarm. In an infrastructure operation context, this function consumes information such as ResourceOrder, ProjectElement.
It can produce indicators - such as severity and risk indicator - or identify constraints or choice element concerning the intervention slot in order to minimize impacts.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Audit -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Audit">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.4.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">148</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Audit The Service Audit Aggregate Function permits to identify and check the consistency of the current state and the configured characteristics of the installed Services, between the Service Repository, Service Platforms and Network Equipment. Its to ensure that ordered and delivered Products / Know-hows are well configured and activated. 
This function can be used after changes of equipment or software for example.

Several mechanisms are available such as :
- Notification 
- Polling - can be automatic or on-demand (in case of Trouble Ticket for example).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Reconciliation -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Reconciliation">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.4.1.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">149</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Reconciliation The Service Reconciliation Aggregate Function defines, suggests to an administrator or directly applies, rules to treat gaps between the installed Service Repository and the current state of installed Services on Service Platforms or equipment, identified by the Service Audit. 
These rules describe in which cases it is necessary to update the Service Repository, via the Service Repository Management, or to update the Service Platform itself (ex: Mobile Line and Voice Mail) through specific operations comparable to delivery ones.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.4.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">150</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Management Service Test Management Aggregate Function functions provide the necessary functionalities to manage the end-to-end execution of a test for a service (test of a service use case getting through all concerned resources, local loop, DSLAM, Service Platform, etc.), either for manual or automatic testing in order to ensure that this service is working properly.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Analysis -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Analysis">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.4.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">151</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Analysis Service Test Analysis Aggregate Function analysis Service Test results in order to propose actions to solve issues and provide reporting.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Conducting -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Conducting">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.4.2.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">152</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Conducting Service Test Conducting Aggregate Function provides the necessary functionalities to manage the end-to-end execution of a test for a service (test of a service use case getting through all concerned resources, local loop, DSLAM, Service Platform, ), either for manual or automatic testing in order to ensure that this service is working properly.
This function is used in the following processes:
- fulfillment for ensuring that the assigned service works as designed by testing the end-to-end service implementation. 
- problem management which can trigger automatic tests during service problem isolation. 
- service supervision, performance &amp; quality management to acquire data necessary to analyze the running services.
Service Test Conducting functionalities include:
- Verifying test authorization and quota management: checks depending on the initiator of the test request if it is authorized and if limitation applies in terms of test quota (linked to resources limitation or other technical constraints)
- Carrying out test priority: the priority of a test for customer care directly in front of a user is higher than for scheduled bulk test campaign
- Managing test scheduling:
  - can be triggered to schedule test (unitary, bulk) regularly (every week, every month)
  - should take into account the impact of the test on the services in order to limit it. For example, a test on a TV service can be programmed at 2 am in order to not disturb the usage of the user.
- Managing bulk test: as well as unitary test with flexibility concerning the criteria (category of service, geographical area, type of customer)
- and setting up the test context and configuration
- Test execution with access to the various data required to perform service testing
  - During the execution of a service test, resource tests can be executed to recover information (like inventory data)  needed to calculate the service test result
  - Data collected during service test can be inventory data (for example in IS inventory) or data related to services coming from service platform and equipment like network equipment or user devices.
  - Test execution produces a test result that may contain a raw result and an enrichment of this result depending on the service test definition and associated rules.
   For example: for a internet test the raw result can be the associated bandwidth and an enrichment can be done depending on the user access type (fiber, xdsl) to evaluate if the result is suitable or not.
- Managing service test scenario: service test management must manage service test scenario with the capacity to trigger additional service tests depending on the results of previous executed tests of the test scenario.
  - In addition to rules, test scenario can be designed to use signature templates data to identify the existence of disruptive events on services that usually correspond to an aggregation of disruptions on resources like water on cable, electrical interferences with other equipment</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Repository Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Repository Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.4.2.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">153</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Repository Management Service Test Repository Management Aggregate Function manages tests of services including the scheduling, setting up the test configuration, acquisition and management of test resources, test results interpretation, reporting of test results and close down of the test of the service.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Reporting -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Test Reporting">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.4.2.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">154</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Test Reporting Service Test Reporting reports the raw test results that may depends on the test execution context data.

	- For the same test, depending on the initiator, the result restitution can be more or less detailed. Ex: Customer care needs more and more details with higher care level.
	- However test result restitution levels must always be mutually consistent through all the execution context
</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Configuration & Activation -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Configuration & Activation">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.5.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">155</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Configuration & Activation Service Configuration &amp; Activation is in charge of 

	- configuring Know-Hows (Customer Facing Service) and Technical Solutions (Resource Facing Service) taking into account constraints between them and applying engineering rules.
	-  and activating and pre-activating Services.
</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Activation -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Activation">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.5.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">156</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Activation Service Activation Aggregate Function is in charge of activating and pre-activating Services.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Configuration -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Configuration">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.5.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">157</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Configuration Service Configuration Aggregate Function is in charge of configuring Know-Hows (CustomerFacingService) and Technical Solutions (ResourceFacingService) taking into account constraints between them and applying engineering rules.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Order Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Order Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.5.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">158</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Order Management Service Order Management (SOM) Aggregate Function manages the end to end lifecycle of a Service Order request, from its configuration up to the technical delivery on network equipment and service platforms.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Availability -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Availability">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.5.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">159</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Availability The Service Availability Aggregate Function validates that the service or services specified on the Service Order, or necessary regarding the product items specified in the Customer Order are:
- available at the specified customer/service location 
- feasible from a network and/or service platforms point of view
- compatible with related existing services - with or without changing their characteristics or technical solution
It includes Technical Feasibility (line type characteristics, coverage, equipment features ), and Technical Compatibility with the resources configurations already existing to support current customer installed services (refer to the Resource Availability function).

It can identify the need 
- for changing the current technical solution to respond to the order - according to allowed operations declared at catalogue level and engineering rules (Example: for an existing VOIP service, change H323 solution to SIP solution to add a new service such as Stop Secret)
- for modifying existing installed Products characteristics to maintain global integrity of a set of inter-depending services (Example: when changing the installed address of a fixed access, it can be necessary to change the call number of the related voice service, if it depends on geography)
- for ordering pre-requisite customer equipment (Example: the availability for broadband access is confirmed but the only possible - or preferred - solution is fiber, and its necessary to install an ONT and to simultaneously order a compatible box)

It also determines the standard delivery time needed, taking into account 
- resources availability
- the need of human intervention at any point of the network - and time and skills necessary to plan it
If an intervention is needed at the delivery address, regarding network context, it specifies it to the customer order capture, to allow it to manage an appointment.

To be noticed: if an intervention at the delivery address is always mandatory (or never necessary) for an ordered product, this information can be managed directly at product catalogue level.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Order Completion -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Order Completion">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.5.2.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">160</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Order Completion The service Order Completion Aggregate Function permits to enrich the CFS Order with information useful for the update of the installed resources, but not directly part of the ordered service (CFS).
Example: credentials to deliver a VOIP line, or IMSI to deliver a mobile line.
It can also describe necessary actions on other CFS, to maintain global integrity of a set of inter-depending services. 
Example: update the CFS Stop Secret - at least one of the related installed resources - when changing the call number of the CFS VOIP.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Order Initialization -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Order Initialization">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.5.2.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">161</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Order Initialization The Service Order Initialization Aggregate Function permits to:
- Identify the CFS specification and configuration related to the ordered product, in a context of customer order delivery
- Check if a corresponding CFS is operational in the service repository, and so determine the operation at CFS level (creation or modification)
- Initiate a service (CFS) order with these information
- Eventually group in the same service order several ordered product, based on the same CFS specification, and/or identified to be delivered at the same time by the Customer Order Orchestration.

This function can also permit to initialise CFS Orders for operator internal needs, for example to change (part of) a technical solution and migrate operational CFS on the new solution elements (ex: VOIP H323 -&gt; VOIP SIP).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Order Repository Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Order Repository Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.5.2.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">162</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Order Repository Management Service Order Repository Management Aggregate Function tracks and manages the events and the lifecycle related to the Service (CFS) Order and to its items (e.g.: service order lines).
It gathers Service Order items delivery events from Service Order Orchestration and manages related Service Order and CFS lifecycles (via the Service Management Aggregate Function).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Order Orchestration -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Order Orchestration">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.5.2.5</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">163</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Order Orchestration The Service Order Orchestration function provides workflow and orchestration capabilities for a dedicated Service (CFS) Order.
Orchestration is needed when:
- the technical solution includes the update of the operator Installed Resources and the purchase of a partner product (ex: local loop purchase)
- a work order is necessary at the delivery address or somewhere in the operator network
- part of the delivery process or checks are delegated to another SOM (and factory) (ex : check access or update it)
- contributing or support systems have to be informed (ex : Karma for the box)

Example: to deliver a VOIP service, it will orchestrate actions on Access Network Factory (to check access compatibility), VOIP service platform and CPE.

Service Order Orchestration will also orchestrate and manage dependencies between related Service Order items of Service Order.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Technical Solution Identification -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Technical Solution Identification">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.5.2.6</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">164</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Technical Solution Identification The Service Technical Solution Identification Aggregate Function identifies the technical solution to apply (RFS Specification and/or Supplier Product Specification) to deliver the ordered service (CFS) - up to the resources types needed (Resources Specification) and related operations - according to: 
- the configuration and/or location of the ordered service (CFS),
- the articulation between CFS and RFS described in the Service specification repository,
- the articulation between CFS operations and RFS operations described in the Service specification repository,
- the potential engineering rules of choice if several RFS can fit in with the CFS, as described in the Service specification repository, 
- the potential constraints related to the existing - or simultaneously ordered - customer equipment type,
- the services and resources repository.

The service inventory keeps the links between the CFS and each chosen element of the technical solution - RFS specification and resource specification or Supplier Product specification.
This function can also be used in a study phase for the design of non standard services, mainly for B2B market needs, and the definition of a new technical solution, more often by assembling elements of existing technical solutions (and adding specific equipments).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Installed Resources Identification -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Installed Resources Identification">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.5.2.7</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">165</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Installed Resources Identification Installed Resources Identification Aggregate Function identifies the installed resources to update, as part of the chosen technical solution, to deliver the ordered service (CFS).
This information enrich the CFS order and the CFS.

This choice is based on service catalogue rules (between RFS specification and Resources specification) and it can be necessary to check the installed resources availability, occupancy,  via the Resource Availability function.

Cloud example: In case of a cloud service, the Service Order Delivery process only identifies the equipment in charge of the management of the cloud infrastructure - and it will be informed of the ordered service characteristics. Depending on its own rules, the cloud infrastructure manager will decide or not to immediately allocate and configure all - or part of - the required resources.
So the first usage request of the cloud service can trigger the effective choice and configuration of the necessary resources - and these resources can change between usages. In this case the end of the delivery process is assumed by the manager of the cloud infrastructure. 

Remark: This function is globally a part of the technical solution identification.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Order Delivery Preparation -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Order Delivery Preparation">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.5.2.8</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">166</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Order Delivery Preparation The Service Order Delivery Preparation Aggregate Function allows in the context of a Service Order to:
- query catalogues and installed bases to identify what needs to be delivered: resource specification and its configuration, service specification (CFSSpec), intervention (WorkSpec), supplier product, related to the ordered service,
- prepare a Resource Order (CFS Order), a Service Order which will be delegated to another system, a Supplier Order, Stock Item Order or Work Order with the necessary information (the effective update in the order repositories will be supported by the corresponding Order Inventory Management functions)

In the case of a Service associated with an existing Internal Resource Type, this function also allows to: 
- check if a corresponding Installed Resource is operational in the resource installed base, and determine the operation to be performed at Resource level (creation or modification)
- eventually group in the same Resource order several ordered services, based on the same CFS specification, and/or identified to be delivered at the same time by the Service Order Delivery Orchestration.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Delivery Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Delivery Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.5.3.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">168</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Delivery Management Service Delivery Management AF groups functions involved in delivering Service Instances (such as the stream of a video, managing a customer's instance on an online game, operating a Platform-as-a-Service virtual machine, etc.).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Problem Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Problem Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.6.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">169</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Problem Management Service Problem Management groups functions in charge of managing Service Trouble.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Problem Repository Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Problem Repository Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.6.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">170</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Problem Repository Management Service Problem Repository Management Aggregate Function includes:
- Service Trouble Ticket recording
- Initialization of the Service Trouble - or association to an existing Service Trouble in case of collective disturbance
- Service Trouble Ticket lifecycle management
- Reporting</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Problem Resolution -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Problem Resolution">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.6.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">171</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Problem Resolution The Service Problem Resolution Aggregate Function allows to resolve the service problem in order to bring back the service to a normal operational state as efficiently as possible.
This may involve automated or manual restoration activities. It may also involve reconfiguration, re-assignments, temporary work-arounds, and/or dispatches of field technicians.
These resolution actions can be temporary or palliative, to quickly stop the trouble, and followed by other actions which will definitely treat the trouble cause. So different resolution actions can be planned and executed, associated to the same Service Trouble.
These actions can be treated through resource trouble ticket - and complementary analysis at resource level - or directly through resource order (ex: reconfiguration of an equipment).
For example to resolve a Service Trouble, the action could be to position the service on another resource (new resource order), if the current installed base resource is defective.

The Service Problem Resolution Aggregate Function includes:
- Identification of the diagnostic actions relevant to identify the service trouble, based on the service type and rules defined by the Service Problem Strategy and Policy Management function
- Triggering of these diagnostic actions and analysis of their results
- Initialization of the Service Trouble - or association to an existing Service Trouble in case of collective disturbance
- Identification of the internal actor or team in charge of the Service Trouble Ticket management, follow up and reporting
- Escalation procedure.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Problem Policy Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Problem Policy Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.6.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">172</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Problem Policy Management Service Problem Policy Management Aggregate Function allows to manage the service problem typology, actions and rules, describing:
- all the types of known troubles related to a service type  
- the actions and rules of triggering, of linking and of results analysis that define the strategies for identifying
   - the trouble associated to a - or a set of - service trouble ticket(s) 
   - the cause(s) of this trouble and the actions to do to resolve the service trouble . 

The definition of the trouble (with specification and rules) is very important because it defines the sequence logic of the actions to be realized to identify and qualify the trouble, one or several associated causes , and to identify the best solution to solve the problem. Specifications and rules may take into account characteristics like urgency level, impact level, priority level of the service trouble ticket.

The different action types are:
- Diagnostic actions and rules to identify the service trouble 
   - For example: simple Service test or Service test scenario, inventories consultation to identify installed service and associated installed resources, test historic consultation, 
- Diagnostic actions  and rules to identify the service trouble cause(s) and the possible root cause
   - For example: Plan workforce intervention to check an equipment, launch test scenario, query QoS historic, followed by service test launch if the installed service has a decreasing QoS, query service usage historic, service platforms logs, equipments logs, anomaly historic, 
- Definition of the logic of interpretation of the results of these diagnostic actions, to identify resolution actions. These resolution actions can be temporary or palliative, to quickly stop the trouble, and followed by other actions which will definitely treat the trouble cause. 
   - Example: to immediately solve an internet access break, we can provide the customer with a specific equipment which permits wireless connection on 3G/4G network - so he can wait for its internet access repair. 
- Definition of notification action, depending on the trouble or the trouble cause identified, for example display a message on the CSP web site, or broadcast a vocal message on IVR in case of collective trouble.
- Definition of lead action, mainly the need or not to initialize ProductTrouble Tickets for all the installed product concerned by the service trouble.

These different actions will be authorized - or not - depending on actors role and associated rights. 

It is also possible to define rules to manage Service Trouble Tickets and Service Trouble priorities, depending on SLA products subscribed (or assigned by regulatory authorities, to hospitals, strategic installations, ).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Trouble Analysis -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Trouble Analysis">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.6.1.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">173</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Trouble Analysis Service Trouble Analysis function permits to identify
- the service trouble cause via diagnostic actions (Test, inventories and historics consultation, Workforce Intervention) and interpretation rules of their results
- the necessary resolution actions - or the best identified solution - to realize to solve the problem.

The identification of the service trouble cause can be made through:
- Analysis of associated Service Trouble Ticket Information (first diagnostic)
- Analysis of Service alarm events (fault of performance data )
- Issuing service tests, including one time tests or a series of tests over a period of time
- Analysis of previous service test results , service usage historic, QoS historic, service platform logs, equipment logs, anomaly historics, etc.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Trouble Tracking and Business Value Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Trouble Tracking and Business Value Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.6.1.5</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">174</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Trouble Tracking and Business Value Development Service Trouble Tracking and Lifecycle Management Aggregate Function tracks and manages the events and the lifecycle related to the Service Trouble (for example when the resolution is ended), and assures that service troubles are assigned, coordinated, efficiently solved, and escalating as needed.

It is also in charge of grouping Service Troubles with the same Trouble Cause and of grouping the related Service Trouble Tickets (for example, in case of a collective disturbance).

It manages Service Trouble closure according to business rules:

	- the service trouble can be closed as soon as a palliative action has been done and the user retrieved a not degraded use - at least one Resource Trouble Ticket is still ongoing
	- or only when the trouble is definitely treated - all associated Resource Trouble Tickets are closed.
</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.6.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">175</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Quality and Performance Management The Service Quality and Performance Management Aggregated Function groups functions that supervise service functioning and manage resulting alarms.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Analysis -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Analysis">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.6.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">176</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Quality and Performance Analysis The Service Quality and Performance Analysis Aggregate Function assures that service alarms produced by the Service Quality &amp; Performance Supervision function are correctly handled, affected and distributed and escalated as needed, depending on rules defined by Service Supervision Strategy &amp; Policy Management. 

The service alarms can concern 
- a bad functioning of a service (unsuccessful communications)
- the reach of a quality or performance threshold
- or a degradation of the performance or quality of service.

 Service Quality and Performance Analysis Aggregate Function includes:
- service Alarm qualification (severity level determination, enrichment with data from Service Installed Base
- filtering: if the service alarm concerns a service based on a resource currently under a scheduled work, it can be inhibited
- correlation: for example several alarms can be associated each other based on engineering rules and reduced to one root alarm on the service
- assignment and distribution of service qualified alarms if necessary to a responsible actor.

If the service alarm is validated and requires a search for cause and a triggering of action, a service trouble ticket is created and will be handled by trouble mechanism. When the Service Trouble is resolved, the Service Alarm could be, automatically or not, deactivated or its severity level decreased.

In other cases the alarm can be handled, without creating an associated trouble ticket, by following exploitation forms, with possibly triggering of manual actions.
In every cases, it could be closed manually by an assigned actor.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Repository Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Repository Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.6.2.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">177</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Quality and Performance Repository Management Service Quality and Performance Repository Management Aggregate Function collects and manages Service quality and performance from Resource quality and performance and from Service tests.
It is also in charge of reporting Service Quality and Performance.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Supervision -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Quality and Performance Supervision">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.6.2.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">178</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Quality and Performance Supervision The Service Quality and Performance Supervision Aggregate Function enables service functioning to be measured (in real-time or not), and supervised, depending on rules defined by Service Quality and Performance Strategy Development, in order to prevent the service quality and performance degradation, with an on-the-fly analysis. 

It includes: 
- filtering, aggregation of the collected information
- analysis of 
     - different types of information directly issued from equipment 
          - examples: continuity check information, IP ping, time to access Internet services transmitted by probes, 
     - results of tests or historic of tests
     - service usage
     - traffic degradations. 
- it compares these information with expected values or pre-established limits
     - when there is a great augmentation of service use and traffic congestion it can trigger an alarm that will be handled via alarm &amp; problem management to set up a load-shedding mechanism (example for new year SMS).
- enrich or calculate service quality and performance indicators (includes KQI, KPI) 
     - Examples of KQI: time to repair warranty, availability percentage on a time slot, percentage of correct vocal quality communications
- it compares these indicators with expected values or pre-established limits
- analysis and evaluation of service performance indicators evolution
- it enables the quality of services to be analyzed by means of aggregation, enrichment, and correlation of performance data.
- it produces Service Alarm 
     - in case of performance degradation (for example when a predefined threshold is reached in application of the rules defined at the strategy level)
     - in case of SLA violations 
- it manages (generation/visualization/distribution) the reports resulting of QoS analysis.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Usage Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Usage Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.7.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">179</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Usage Management Service Usage Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of collecting, identifying, aggregating and assigning Service Usage records.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Usage Guiding -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Service Usage Guiding">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">4.7.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">180</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Service Usage Guiding The Service Usage Guiding Aggregate Function ensures resource usage records aggregation (consolidation, elimination of duplicates) to generate service usage records ("global usage repor" often called Charging Data Record or CDR).
Each service usage record is associated to the installed service that generated it (ex: mobile line service).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Strategic Analysis -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Strategic Analysis">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">181</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Strategic Analysis Resource Strategic Analysis Aggregated Function groups functions useful to conduct delayed analysis, on the collected information concerning resource, which may have an influence on the strategy.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Infrastructure Analysis -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Infrastructure Analysis">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.1.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">182</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Infrastructure Analysis Resource Infrastructure Analysis Aggregate Function analysis Network infrastructure for identifying optimum network deployments, identifying strategic sites and Network sizing needs.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Supply Chain Planning Analysis -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Supply Chain Planning Analysis">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.1.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">183</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Supply Chain Planning Analysis Supply Chain Planning Analysis Aggregate Function identifies sourcing forecast and plans sourcing taking into account current inventory, in collaboration with all suppliers.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Performance Analysis -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Performance Analysis">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.1.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">184</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Performance Analysis The Resource Performance Analysis Aggregate Function comprises off-line analysis of the resource performance and forecast concerning the resource performance indicators, depending on rules and indicators defined through Resource Supervision Strategy and Policy Management.

It calculates resource performance indicators (aggregated or not) and produces statistics and forecasts concerning the resource performance (ex: loads linked to the uses, number of failures, ), based on the following information: 
- indicators already calculated by the Resource Performance Supervision function
- aggregated resource usages 
- historic of resource test
- resource trouble or resource trouble ticket
- resource alarm
- data directly issued from equipment - all of them are not analyzed on the fly.

The information produced can also be used to improve the rules and indicators definition of Resource Supervision Policy Management.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Diagnostic and Test Quality Loop -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Diagnostic and Test Quality Loop">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.1.1.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">185</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Diagnostic and Test Quality Loop Resource Diagnostic and Test Quality Loop Aggregate Function provides the necessary functionalities to manage a quality loop on diagnostic to continually tweak and improve it.
Functionalities include:
- Management of success/failure result feedback of diagnostic and correction provided by technicians or the systems after a correction
- Offline analysis of the success/failure data compared to the diagnostic and correction rules to provide modifications to improve them. This analysis is done using statistical diagnostic data and test results to evaluate their relevance and the needed updates.
The provided improvements can be used to update the definition of diagnostic rules and correction actions in the Resource Trouble Policy Management function and the definition of tests in the Resource Test Policy Management function.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Strategy Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Strategy Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">186</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Strategy Management Resource Strategy Management aggregate function groups functions useful to define the resource strategy and to develop it (new resources or enhance the existing resources).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Strategy Design and Planning -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Strategy Design and Planning">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.1.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">187</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Strategy Design and Planning The Resource Strategy, Design and Planning is responsible for the definition of the resource strategy at the company level. This function takes into account a list of inputs to determine the need to implement new resources, to modify or replace existing resources or to remove them. As inputs, the function can use the following information: 
- The existing context of the company with available services and resources (network, PFS)
- The data produced by the Resource Usage Analysis function and traffic and geographical evolution analyses
- The performance and test results of existing resources or in test resources (like POC to validate a new technology)
- The new technologies that are developed in the industry 
- Marketing constraints concerning the date of opening for a new service that requires evolution at the resource level 
- The cost of the existing and new technologies with benefit evaluation 
- The possibility to share resources with third parties/partner 
- The service strategy already defined in a top down process (service driven)

Following the definition of the service and resource strategy the Resource Strategy, Design and Planning function updates the resource design by requesting the modifications (creating, updating, deleting) of the resource specifications to the resource catalogue and thus the evolution of the available technology solutions.

Finally, the Resource Strategy, Design and Planning function provides a high level planning of the resource strategy implementation taking into account the service strategy plan. The strategy planning can take into account constraints (like legal constraints or the need of validation by authorities) and notify other companies, local authorities or governments of the chosen strategy, for example the choice of FTTH technology versus VDSL.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Capability Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Capability Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">188</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Capability Management Resource Capability Management Aggregate Function groups functions in charge of specifying and managing Resource Specification capabilities.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Specification Capability Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Specification Capability Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.2.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">189</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Specification Capability Development Resource Capability Specification Management Aggregate Function manages Resource Capability Specifications for all Resource Specifications. The capability specifications represent the general, common and invariant characteristics of resource that may be realized in more than one type of specific resource. Examples of capability are Layer2, Data, radio and Transport.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Deployment and Retirement -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Deployment and Retirement">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.2.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">190</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Deployment and Retirement The Resource Deployment and Retirement Aggregate Function is responsible for implementing the resource strategy high level plan and develop the resource infrastructure (network, PFS, IT...). The Resource Deployment and Retirement function manages the deployment constraints like area limitation (for test or for marketing purposes), progressive deployment following precise milestones These constrains may be provided from the service level.

The resource deployment and retirement can be responsible to manage constraints (like legal constraints or the need of validation by authorities) and notify other companies, local authorities or governments of the effective deployment or retirement of resources like the implementation of FTTH technology in a building.

The Resource Installed Base is updated and the resources are pre-configured and pre-activated if needed, for example for non-customer specific parameters, via the Resource Order Initialization and Installed Resource Management functions.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Resources Check -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Resources Check">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.2.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">191</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Resources Check Resource Test Resources Check Aggregate Function is in charge of defining, implementing and checking resources used to test Resources.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.2.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">192</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Network Development Network Development AF groups functions that acquire, expand, improve and maintain communications networks.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Planning and Building -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Planning and Building">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.2.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">193</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Network Planning and Building Network Planning and Building AF groups functions responsible for ensuring network capacity is available to meet the needs of Customer service requests and ensuring network restorability. Network capacity includes the access network and the various OSI Layer 1-4+ core backbone networks. Network Planning and Building functions manage network capacity with the goals of (1) maximizing return from capital expenditures, (2) ensuring inventory and financial accuracy, (3) providing capacity to meet customer demands, (4) future-proofing evolving technology options, (5) best leveraging of supply-chain management, (6) setting up the OSS data to permit flow-through automation once network elements have been made available in OSS inventory.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Access Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Access Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.2.2.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">194</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Network Access Management Network Access Management AF groups functions that investigate and implement alternatives to building network.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Change Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Change Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.2.2.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">195</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Network Change Management The Network Change Management AF groups functions that notify and communicate to concerned parties about changes to the communications network and its elements - planned changes, changes happening in real-time, and past changes (for root cause analysis) - in order to reduce the "blast radius" of such changes</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Specification Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Specification Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.3.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">196</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Specification Management The Resource Specification Management Aggregated Function functions enable to create and update the catalogue at Resource level, for all types of resources (ResourceSpecification):
- Physical Resources (ex: cable, passive equipment, )
- Logical Resources (ex: phone number, IP address, APPS such as Orange TV, Orange Maps, )
- Compound Resources (ex: DSLAM, box, )
It manages the links with the Business Partner Product Catalogue for Resources specification that correspond to equipment rented to a supplier (devices, network equipment, hardware &amp; software, ).
It also manages the links to the Skill catalogue to identify for each type of resource - or of operation on a resource - which type of skill is necessary to make the intervention.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Allocation Policy Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Allocation Policy Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.3.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">197</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Allocation Policy Development The Resource Allocation Policy Development Aggregate Function enables to define and to check consistency of potentially complex rules to automate the choice of resource type and/or instance during different processes such as delivery management, assurance, infrastructure management, . 

Criteria can be of various nature and combined:
- geographical (for fixed phone number allocation, )
- defined by regulation authorities which allocate the resources to the Service Provider (range of MSISDN dedicated to M2M, IMSI allocation rules to SIM cards depending of future vendor - the Service Provider itself, MVNO,  - , duration of freezing before using again a phone number, )
- depending on the configured characteristics of the Installed Service / Resource to deliver (ex: phone number allocated during Customer Order Capture, internet speed required, ) or associated to it (ex: QoS or SLA)
- depending on the current state of Service Platforms, Network equipment (occupation rate, load balancing,  )
- depending on  Supplier/Partner contracts (volume discounts, )</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Specification Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Specification Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.3.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">198</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Specification Development The Resource Specification Development Aggregate Function enables to create and update the catalogue at Resource level, for all types of resources (Resource Specification):
- Physical Resources (ex: cable, passive equipment, )
- Logical Resources (ex: phone number, IP address, APPS such as TV, Maps, )
- Compound Resources (ex: DSLAM, box, )
Examples of Resource Specification:
- iphone 5S and its characteristics such as colors available, size and weight, capacity, 
- DSLAM Alcatel XXX and its characteristics such as size and weight, electricity consumption, number of DSL ports, 

It manages the links with the Supplier/Partner Product Catalogue for Resources specification that correspond to equipment rented to a supplier (devices, network equipments, hardware &amp; software, ).
It also manages the links to the Skill catalogue to identify for each type of resource - or of operation on a resource - which type of skill is necessary to make the intervention.

It also permits to describe in a short way resources types that as a Service Provider we dont own or commercialize (ex: mobile phones used by our customers).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Policy and Schedule Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Policy and Schedule Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.3.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">199</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Policy and Schedule Management Resource Test Policy and Schedule Management Aggregate Function provides the necessary functionalities to manage the resource test catalogue containing the resource test specifications, the rules that define the test configuration setting up based on the context, the rules that define the strategies for executing the test as well as how the test results should be restituted.
Test strategies can range from simple to complex and encompass the definition of test scenario.
The definition of the test (with specification and rules) is very important because it defines the question that is asked and thus shapes the result that is reported back.
The functionalities include providing:
- Resource Test Specification and test scenario management
  - Test scenario management: for example, in case of redundant resources the test scenario can test the default resources involved in a service before testing one of the possible redundant resources solutions available and not necessarily test all the other existing solutions if the previous tests are positives.
  - Managing resource test capabilities: some resources have limited capabilities for executing tests, especially for multiple parallel testing. Resources can be either the resources involved for the user or test related resources like probes &amp; robots.
  - Managing initiator authorization: some tests are restricted to some user roles (internal or external) so the authorizations are defined at the Resource Test Strategy and Policy Management.
- Test rules defining the strategies for setting up test configuration
- Test rules defining how to carrying out the resource test
  - For example: to choose the next test in a scenario depending on the results of the previous tests
- Test rules defining the enrichment of test results. 
  - For example: the enrichment of CPU &amp; disk space usage compared to the defined strategy of the company
  - For example: the enrichment of copper line electrical conductivity compared to previously measure template
- Test rules defining the restitution of the result depending on test execution context
   - For example: a test result of ok/not ok can be enough for a user whereas technicians require more details in the result of the test.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Supervision Policy Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Supervision Policy Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.3.1.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">200</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Supervision Policy Development The Resource Supervision Policy Development Aggregate Function enables to define the policy concerning supervision (indicators and alarm) at Resource level.

It includes: 
- Definition of indicators (measures) associated with the resource type, with thresholds, calculation rules and consequences
     - examples of indicators with threshold : pressure, temperature 
     - examples of indicators with calculation rules : storage occupation rate, load rate
- Definition of rules 
     - for alarm activation
          - for example if a dysfunction is detected in its power supply, or in the functioning of the elements which constitutes it (card, port, internal ventilator) and also if its temperature exceeds a threshold.
     - for a Trouble ticket creation next to a resource alarm qualification.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Policy Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Policy Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.3.1.5</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">201</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Trouble Policy Management Resource Trouble Policy Management Aggregate Function allows to manage the resource problem typology, actions and rules describing:
- all the types of known troubles related to resource type  
- the actions and rules of triggering, of linking and of results analysis that define the strategies for identifying  
   - the trouble associated to a - or a set of - resource trouble ticket(s)
   - the cause(s) of this trouble and the actions to do to resolve the resource trouble. 

The definition of the trouble (with specification and rules) is very important because it defines the sequence logic of the actions to be realized to identify and qualify the problem, one or several associated causes , and to identify the best solution to solve the problem. Specifications and rules may take into account characteristics like urgency level, impact level, priority level of the resource trouble ticket.

The different action types are:
- Diagnostic actions and rules to identify the resource trouble. This step permits to identify the installed resource(s) at fault
   - For example: simple resource test or resource test scenario, inventories consultation to identify installed resource and expected configuration characteristics, test historic consultation, 
- Diagnostic actions  and rules to identify the resource trouble cause(s) and the possible root cause . This step permits to identify why the related installed resource(s) is (are) at fault.
   - For example: Plan workforce intervention to check an equipment, launch resource test scenario, query QoS historic, followed by resource test launch if the installed resource has a decreasing QoS, query resource usage historic, equipments logs, anomaly historic, .
- Definition of the logic of interpretation of the results of these diagnostic actions, to identify resolution actions. These resolution actions can be temporary or palliative, to quickly stop the trouble, and followed by other actions which will definitely treat the trouble cause 
   - For example: if a network cable is cut, we can first decide to migrate all or part of the interrupted installed services on another cable (through dedicated resource orders) to quickly solve the trouble, and secondly initiate a project - often called scheduled work - to repair the cut cable. After the final repair, its not always necessary to migrate the installed service back.
- Definition of notification action, depending on the trouble or the trouble cause identified, for example display a message on the web site, or broadcast a vocal message on IVR in case of collective trouble (example: network cable cut, power cut, ) 
- Definition of lead action, mainly the need or not to initialize Service Trouble Tickets for all the installed services concerned by the resource trouble.

These different actions will be authorized - or not - depending on actors role and associated rights. 
For example we could authorized the customer (or user) to directly diagnose a configuration problem on his box and to trigger the upload of a new software version, but reserve the capacity of updating the configuration of a service platform to support level 2 actors.

It is also possible to define rules to manage Resource Trouble Tickets and Resource Trouble priorities, depending on criteria such as:
- Priority of the related Service Trouble Ticket(s) or Service Trouble(s) 
- Number of Product Trouble Tickets that can be expected, linked to the usage of the installed resource.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Supply Chain Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Supply Chain Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
