<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Work Specification Development Work Specification Development Aggregate Function enables to specify types of Work including required skills and resources to realize human activities such as training or resource installation.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">206</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Management Resource Management groups functions in charge of managing the inventory of resources.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Location Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Location Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">207</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Location Management The Location Management Aggregate Function permits to describe, identify and manage any type of location useful for the Service Provider operational processes.
It includes:
- geographical address description (city, street, number in the street, ), described using national or international codifications (for streets, countries, etc.),
- buildings description (floors, rooms, racks, ), 
- geospatial information,
-Information such as contact methods, opening time, access code, warnings, etc. can also be defined.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Regulated Logical Resources Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Regulated Logical Resources Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">208</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Regulated Logical Resources Management The Regulated Logical Resources Management Aggregate Function permits to describe and manage logical resources such as phone numbers, IP addresses, IMSI, frequencies  which are usually allocated to the Service Provider by regulation authorities, and often in limited quantities.
Specific rules defined by regulation authorities are treated by the Resource Allocation Policy Management function.
The Service Provider also often has specific operations to manage when these resources are allocated or returned, and fair usage reporting to ensure.
For example, this function covers Portability constraints for phone numbers, which oblige the Service Provider to always know which operator uses a phone number to be able to manage changes of operator and network routing.
It can also cover constraints related to mail address management.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Allocation -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Allocation">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">209</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Allocation The Resource Reconciliation Aggregate Function defines, suggests to an administrator or directly applies rules to treat gaps between the Installed Resource Base and the current state or characteristics of Installed Resources identified by the Audit - Resource Discovery function. 

These rules describe in which cases it is necessary to update the Installed Resource Base (ex: notification of a new equipment), via the Installed Resource Management function, or to update the equipment itself (ex: box)  - in some cases it can be necessary  
- to trigger again the configuration of the installed resource 
- or even to trigger human intervention (ex: gap on physical network)</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Audit and Discovery -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Audit and Discovery">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.1.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">210</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Audit and Discovery The Audit - Resource Discovery Aggregate Function permits to identify and check the consistency of the current state and the configured characteristics of the Installed Resources, between the installed resources base (or inventory) and the equipments. Several mechanisms are available such as:
- Notification (concern active equipment, for example box, OLT, mobile phone really used, )
- Polling (concern passive equipment). Polling can be automatic or on-demand (in case of Trouble Ticket for example), direct or through intermediate equipment. For example, to identify home equipment, information is collected through the box.
- Report by technicians in the field

The installed resources identified by the Audit-Resource Discovery function can be:
- elements of the Service Provider Infrastructure
- devices sold or rented to customers by the Service Provider
- devices owned by customers or users, not sold or rented by the Service Provider, and directly or not connected to its network.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Repository Reconciliation -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Repository Reconciliation">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.1.5</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">211</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Repository Reconciliation The Resource Repository Reconciliation Aggregate Function defines, suggests to an administrator or directly applies rules to treat gaps between the Installed Resource Base and the current state or characteristics of Installed Resources identified by the Audit - Resource Discovery function. 

These rules describe in which cases it is necessary to update the Installed Resource Base (ex: notification of a new equipment), via the Installed Resource Management function, or to update the equipment itself (ex: box)  - in some cases it can be necessary  
- to trigger again the configuration of the installed resource 
- or even to trigger human intervention (ex: gap on physical network)</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Repository Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Repository Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.1.6</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">212</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Repository Management The Resource Repository Management Aggregate Function stores and maintains a reference of all technical resources (physical, logical and compound resources). 
The Resource base describes in particular network and IT resources 
- created and eventually configured during the Infrastructure Management process,
- allocated and configured during the Service Delivery process,
- or during Assurance process 
Resources (physical or compound) are linked to their geographical location, when it is relevant and stable. 
Example: DSLAM XXX is located 2 street Louis Armstrong 75015 Paris, building A, floor 2, room 214
Resources can be identified and referenced before any allocation to a service (and/or a product) - and can exist without being used, by a service for example.
Examples:
- devices managed in the Service Provider stocks
- fixed access
- new DSLAM or Service Platform
It also permits to describe in a short way resources that as a Service Provider we dont own or commercialize (ex: mobile phones used by our customers) but that we need to know or to act on. For example when someone arrives in a foreign country with his mobile phone, his SIM card is updated for roaming management (as hosting network).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Simulation -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Simulation">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.1.7</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">213</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Simulation Resource Simulation Aggregate Function provides capacity to design Resource infrastructure and helps analyzing potential changes.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Mediation -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Mediation">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.1.8</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">214</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Mediation Resource Mediation Aggregate Function provides capacity to exchange data with Resources.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Geographical Association -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Geographical Association">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.1.9</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">215</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Geographical Association Resource Geographical Association Aggregate Function includes functions that relate Resources to points or boundaries as identified by geographical references. Geographical references could be as defined in GIS systems, or as defined by external parties or agencies (such as postal codes, emergency response zones, legal boundaries, etc.).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">216</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Management Resource Test Management provides the necessary functionalities to manage the end-to-end execution of a test for a resource, either for manual or automatic testing in order to ensure that this resource is working properly. It includes:
- Verifying test authorization and quota management
- Carrying out test priority
- Managing test scheduling
- Managing bulk test
- Retrieval of appropriate inventory data and setting up the test context and configuration
- Test execution with access to the various resources required to perform resource testing
- Managing resource test scenario
- Test results restitution
- Test results long term logging for offline data analyses. 
- Possible reporting of test results back to the initiator of the test.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Conducting -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Conducting">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">217</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Conducting Resource Test Conducting provides the necessary functionalities to manage the end-to-end execution of a test for a resource, either for manual or automatic testing in order to ensure that this resource is working properly.

The Resource Test Conducting function is used in the following processes:
- fulfillment, usually triggered within the framework of a test of service, for ensuring that the assigned resource works as designed.
- fault management, which can trigger automatic tests during resource fault isolation.
- resource supervision, performance &amp; quality management to acquire data necessary to analyze the running resources.

Resource Test Conducting functionalities include:
- Verifying test authorization and quota management: resource test management checks depending on the initiator of the test request if it is authorized and if limitation applies in terms of test quota (linked to resources limitation or other technical constraints)
- Carrying out test priority: the priority of a test for technician intervening at the user home is higher than for scheduled bulk test campaign
- Managing test scheduling:
  - Resource Test Conducting can be triggered to schedule test (unitary, bulk) regularly (every week, every month)
  - Resource Test Conducting should take into account the impact of the test on the resources in order to limit it. For example, a remote test on a user STB can be programmed at 2 am in order to not disturb the usage of the user.
- Managing bulk test: resource test management must be able to execute unitary test as bulk test with flexibility concerning the criteria (for a category of resource, geographical area, type of customer)
- Retrieval of appropriate inventory data and setting up the test context and configuration
- Test execution with access to the various resources required to perform resource testing. The test execution produces a test result that may contain a raw result and an enrichment of this result depending on the resource test definition and associated rules.
  - For example: for a copper line test the raw result can be the conductivity of the line  and an enrichment can be done depending on conductivity templates calculated previously to evaluate if the result is suitable or not.
- Managing resource test scenario: resource test management must manage resource test scenario with the capacity to trigger additional resource tests depending on the results of previous executed tests of the test scenario.
  - In addition to rules, test scenario can be designed to use signature templates data to identify the existence of disruptive events on resources like water on cable, electrical interferences with other equipment
- Test results restitution: the restitution of the raw test results may depends on the test execution context data.
  - For the same test, depending on the initiator, the result restitution can be more or less detailed. Customer care or technician (ex: during a turn on test) needs more and more details with higher care level. 
  - However test result restitution levels must always be mutually consistent through all  the execution context
- Test results long term logging for offline data analyses. These analyses are done outside of Resource Test Management.
- Possible reporting of test results back to the initiator of the test.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Inventory Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Inventory Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.2.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">219</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Inventory Management Resource Test Inventory Management Aggregate Function manages Resource Tests designs and results.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Reporting -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Reporting">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.2.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">220</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Reporting Resource Test Reporting Aggregate Function provides reporting of resource test results back to the requester.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Analysis -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Test Analysis">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.2.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">221</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Test Analysis Resource Test Analysis Aggregate Function analysis Resource Tests results and forecast long term error trajectory.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Capacity Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Capacity Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.2.5</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">218</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Network Capacity Management Network Capacity Management AF includes functions that address the near-term management of network asset utilization. This includes: Monitor, provide trend analysis and direct the deployment, use and capacity of network inventory in the 0-12 month timeframe to ensure the right technology is efficiently deployed and positioned to meet the service delivery needs of the market, while minimizing capital and expense. For ports, bandwidth and memory measures, this includes measures such as the current consumption, months to exhaust, and average monthly consumption. Capacity management triggers activation of components to meet the current demand.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Work Order Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Work Order Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">222</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Work Order Management The Work Order Management aggregate functions track and manage the events and the lifecycle related to Work Orders, and assures that they are assigned, coordinated and escalating as needed.
It is especially in charge of initiating re-assignments as needed, based on changing priorities, technician progress on other job assignments, etc.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Work Order Assignment and Plan -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Work Order Assignment and Plan">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.3.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">223</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Work Order Assignment and Plan The Work Order Assignment and Plan Aggregate Function provides the functionality required to assign a WorkOrder (that corresponds to manual tasks) to staff members, and to plan it according to :
- WorkOrder priority and due date
- tasks planned duration and required skills
- tasks location - and related constraints like opening time, security issues,  , and optimization of staff trips
- workload / availability of technicians or other concerned staff members
 
There are two possible levels of assignment of a WorkOrder to a calendar entry :
- first, the Work Order is assigned to a pool calendar, according to location and skills criteria.
- secondly, the Work Order is assigned to one or several actors calendar according to real availability, tour planning (mobility management), specific location issues such as safety (e.g. presence of two people needed in some places due to the height of the equipments, and so on)
In case of subcontracting, the operator only performs the first level of assignment, according to the agreement with the supplier.
Back office activities too are only assigned at team level, and then ensure by team members through the team basket.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Work Order Establishment -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Work Order Establishment">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.3.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">224</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Work Order Establishment The Work Order Establishment Aggregate Function receives requests which imply to plan and assign manual activities to staff in a workforce. This work request can be produced from various systems such as Interaction Management, Order Management, Problem Management, Infrastructure Management, etc.
The activity can correspond to technical tasks, an appointment with a customer for pre-sales or demo, 

This function includes :
- filtering the various requests (several requests may result in a single job to be performed, emitters may send duplicate demands, requested action may already be identified by Problem Management, )
- choice of the carrying out model and decomposing the request into Work Order(s) and Work Order Item(s) - each Work Order describing all the operations to be performed by the same actor type at the same time in the same location.
- adding context information (e.g. location information and associated constraints (opening hours/security issues) and produces Work Order(s) to perform the initial request.

The request can be described by one of the following : 
- Resource Order: The order provides the initial state of the resources and the requested final state/or the operation to be executed on them. The Resource Order may be issued by a Customer Order or a Service Order, by an Internal production process, by a problem management restoration process. 
- Project Item: i.e. a specific task in a planned project such as infrastructure roll-out, software deployment, B2B customer complex installation, preventive maintenance, ...
- Interaction: i.e. any agreement to manage an appointment between a company representative (sales, technician, ) and a customer representative for pre-sales, demo, etc.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Work Order Tracking and Business Value Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Work Order Tracking and Business Value Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.3.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">225</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Work Order Tracking and Business Value Development Work Order Tracking and Business Value Development Aggregate Function tracks and manages the events and the lifecycle related to Work Orders, and assures that they are assigned, coordinated and escalating as needed.

It is especially in charge of initiating re-assignments as needed, based on changing priorities, technician progress on other job assignments, etc.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Performance Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Performance Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.4.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">226</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Network Performance Management The Network Performance Management aggregate function ensures the efficiency and reliability of a CSPs network infrastructure. Its responsibilities can be summarized in the following key points:
1.	Monitoring Network Health: Continuously track network performance and identify issues.
2.	Fault Detection: Quickly detect and diagnose network faults or failures.
3.	Performance Optimization: Optimize network configurations and resources for efficiency.
4.	Bandwidth Management: Ensure proper allocation of bandwidth to meet user needs.
5.	Security Monitoring: Monitor for potential security threats and vulnerabilities.
6.	Capacity Planning: Forecast network growth and plan for scalability.
7.	Reporting and Analytics: Provide data-driven insights for decision-making.
8.	Quality of Service (QoS): Ensure consistent and reliable service quality.
9.	Incident Management: Rapidly respond to and resolve network incidents.
10.	Compliance: Ensure network operations align with regulatory and organizational standards, such as SLAs.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Order Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Order Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">227</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Order Management Resource Order Management functions manage the end to end lifecycle of a Resource Order request.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Availability Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Availability Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.1.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">228</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Availability Management Resource Availability Management Aggregate Function ensures that a resource - or a set of resources - is available to deliver a service (CFS) order for example. It can check:
- the state and current configuration of the resource(s)
- its occupancy
- its location
- for an existing local loop if it is directly reusable from one end to the other, or partially un-built (and where it's necessary to rebuild)

This Aggregate Function can be used to confirm Service Availability (at least one set of needed resources is available) and/or the choice of the resources as part of the technical solution.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Order Completion -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Order Completion">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.1.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">229</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Order Completion The Resource Order Completion Aggregate Function permits to enrich the Resource Order with information needed by the equipment, but which are not part of the description of the asked action and havent to be known by the requester of this action. 
Example: the name of the holder or user may be needed by a service platform to deliver a CFS.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Order Initialization -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Order Initialization">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">230</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Order Initialization A Resource Order describes actions required on resources, and can be initiated in several contexts:
- by a Resource Order Management component to update the equipments required to deliver/activate a CFS, or modify a technical solution element
- by the Supply Chain to answer to the customer order of a goods product
- by the Infrastructure Management process, to install new equipments
- by the Service &amp; Resource Assurance process to diagnose or resolve a trouble</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Order Orchestration -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Order Orchestration">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.1.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">231</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Order Orchestration The Resource Order Orchestration Aggregate Function orchestrates the actions on the involved resources, managing dependencies between them.
Example: for SIP VOIP delivery, depending of the chosen technical solution, the Resource Order Management component can orchestrate the update of the HSS, of the AS BASE and of the ENUM platform.
It manages rollback, for example of the updates if one of the equipments cant be updated, and prepares the notification with the global result of the Resource Order (OK or NOK).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Order Tracking and Business Value Development -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Order Tracking and Business Value Development">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.1.5</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">232</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Order Tracking and Business Value Development Resource Order Tracking and Business Value Development Aggregate Function tracks and manages the events and the lifecycle related to the Resource Order and to its items (e.g.: order lines). 
It gathers Resource Order items delivery events from Resource Order Orchestration and manages related Resource Order lifecycle, and Installed Resources lifecycle (via the Installed Resource Management function).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Supply Chain Fulfillment -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Supply Chain Fulfillment">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">233</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Supply Chain Fulfillment Supply Chain Fulfillment aggregate function groups functions related to stock movement, logistics, inventory control, assembling process.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Stock Inventory Control -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Stock Inventory Control">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">234</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Stock Inventory Control The Stock Inventory Control Aggregate Function performs physical inventory of entire inventory or specific sub inventories and defines its approval cycles.
It includes Inventory Quality Control Management dedicated to the control of quality of product in receiving and return processes.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Stock Inventory Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Stock Inventory Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.2.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">235</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Stock Inventory Management Stock Inventory Management Aggregate Function is in charge of managing Stock Items with the related Resources, stock level and requests and tracks Stock Items with its Resources in Warehouses according to receptions.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Stock Level Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Stock Level Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.2.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">236</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Stock Level Management Stock Level Management Aggregate Function is in charge of Warehouse and partners replenishment identification. 
The replenishment business rules can concern materials provided by suppliers or materials distributed within the company (point of sales or warehouses).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Stock Movements Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Stock Movements Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.2.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">237</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Stock Movements Management The Stock Movements Management Aggregate Function manages items movements (i.e. shipment). It includes transfers and reallocations within the organization or to partners.
It's in charge of shipment realization and follow-up including when it is directly done by a business partner.
Examples: This function is used when devices are delivered in shop, or when an equipment is replaced by a technician during an intervention.
Stock Movements Management also manages the stock of documentation and type of documentation (Commercial catalogue, flyers etc.).</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Logistic Availability -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Logistic Availability">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.2.5</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">238</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Logistic Availability The Logistic Availability Aggregate Function allows to:
- Check the availability of tangible products,
- Get the delivery duration,
- Book/release a tangible product.

For this function, the supply chain uses its own stock and delivery duration, but also the stock and delivery duration of its partners.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Provisioning -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Provisioning">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">239</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Provisioning Resource Provisioning Aggregate Function includes functions that design, set up, assign, configure and activate (or support the design, setup, assignment, configuration and activation) of resource solutions, whether in fulfillment of service orders or maintenance or build-out of the communications network.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Provisioning Monitoring -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Provisioning Monitoring">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.3.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">240</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Provisioning Monitoring Resource Provision Monitoring Aggregate Function includes functions that oversee and track Resource Provisioning activities.  Resource Provisioning includes the design, set up, assign, configure and activate (or support the design, setup, assignment, configuration and activation) of resource solutions, whether in fulfillment of service orders or maintenance or build-out of the communications network.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Configuration -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Configuration">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.3.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">242</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Configuration Resource Configuration Aggregate Function is in charge of configuring Resources taking into account constraints between them and applying engineering rules.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Activation -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Activation">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.3.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">243</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Activation Resource Activation Aggregate Function is in charge of activating and pre-activating Resources.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Provisioning Design -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Provisioning Design">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.5.3.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">241</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Provisioning Design Resource Provisioning Design aggregate function groups functions that support the design of resource-based solutions, whether standard (for implementation of product specifications) or custom (for customer-specific services).  The design identifies what resources will need to provisioned and how.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Performance Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Performance Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.6.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">244</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Performance Management The Resource Performance Management Aggregated Function groups functions that supervise resource functioning and manage resulting alarms.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Alarm Repository Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Alarm Repository Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.6.1.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">245</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Alarm Repository Management Resource Alarm Repository Management Aggregate Function is in charge of managing Resource Alarms including recording, lifecycle management and reporting.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Alarm Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Alarm Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.6.1.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">246</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Alarm Management This Resource Alarm Management Aggregate Function assures that resource alarms produced are correctly handled, affected and distributed and escalated as needed, depending on rules defined by Resource Strategy and Policy Management. 

The resource alarm can concern 
- the functioning of a resource (power, card, temperature dysfunction)
- the reach of a performance threshold 
- or a performance degradation. 

This function includes: 
- qualification: severity level determination, enrichment with data from Resource Installed Base.
- filtering: if the resource alarm concern an equipment currently under a scheduled work, it is inhibited
- correlation: for example several alarms can be associated each other based on engineering rules and reduced to one root alarm on the resource
- assignment and distribution of resource qualified alarms if necessary to a responsible actor.

If the resource alarm is recognized and requires a search for cause and triggering of action, a resource trouble ticket is created and will be handled by trouble mechanism.
When the resource Trouble is resolved, the Resource Alarm could be, automatically or not, deactivated or its severity level decreased.

In other cases the alarm can be handled, without creating an associated trouble ticket, by following exploitation forms, with possibly triggering of manual actions.
In every case, it could be closed manually by an assigned actor.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Performance Supervision -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Performance Supervision">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.6.1.5</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">247</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Performance Supervision The Resource Performance Supervision Aggregate Function enables resource functioning to be measured and supervised (in real-time or not), depending on rules defined by Resource Strategy Management, in order to prevent the resource performance degradation, with an on-the-fly analysis. 

It includes: 
- filtering, aggregation
- analysis of 
     - different types of information directly issued from equipment 
          - examples: usages, logs, flows, 
     -  results of tests or historic of tests
- it compares these information with expected values or pre-established limits
- enrich or calculate resource performance indicators (KPI) 
     - examples: Mean Time to Repair (MTTR), Mean Time Between Failure (MTBF)
- analysis and evaluation of resource performance indicators evolution
- it produces Resource Alarm 
     - when an information doesnt correspond to the expected value
     - threshold correlation: when a predefined threshold is exceeded, it is able to trigger a performance alarm which can be managed by Resource Alarm Management function.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Performance Planning -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Performance Planning">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.6.1.6</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">248</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Performance Planning The Resource Performance Planning AF groups functions optimizing an organization's resources effectively. Their primary responsibilities include:
1.	Forecasting resource demands based on historical data and future needs.
2.	Allocating resources efficiently to meet operational requirements.
3.	Monitoring resource utilization and identifying bottlenecks.
4.	Ensuring cost-effective resource utilization to meet business objectives.
5.	Implementing scheduling and routing techniques to maximize resource efficiency.
6.	Adapting plans long-range and in real-time to address changing demands or disruptions.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.6.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">249</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Trouble Management The Resource Trouble Management Aggregate Function groups functions that supervise resource functioning and manage resource troubles of any kind.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Repository Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Repository Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.6.2.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">250</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Trouble Repository Management </rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Resolution -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Resolution">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.6.2.2</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">251</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Trouble Resolution Resource Trouble Resolution Aggregate Function is responsible for repair or replacement of faulty resources or using redundant equipment to replace the faulty resource after a trouble has been detected, in order to bring back the installed resource to a normal operational state as efficiently as possible.  
This may involve automated or manual restoration activities. It may also involve reconfiguration, and/or dispatches of field technicians.
These resolution actions can be temporary or palliative, to quickly stop the trouble, and followed by other actions which will definitely treat the trouble cause (ex: when a telegraph pole falls it can be first strengthened then replaced). So different resolution actions can be planned and executed, associated to the same Trouble. 
When we try palliative action and notice that it doesnt solve the trouble, its not always necessary or interesting to undo it (ex: change of copper pair).
These actions can be treated through resource order(s) (ex: reconfiguration of an equipment, installation of a new equipment) or project management (ex: scheduled work) when several tasks have to be planned and coordinated.
Remark: If the resolution action is a Resource Order, this will be created via the Resource Order Initialization function.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Ticket Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Ticket Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.6.2.3</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">252</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Trouble Ticket Management A Resource Trouble Ticket can be created 
- further to a Product Trouble Ticket resulting from the qualification of a problem indicated by a customer on a material product, 
- or to a Service Trouble Ticket when the resource(s) responsible for the service trouble is (are) identified
- or following resource validated alarms  
- or QoS decreasing validated report

The Resource Trouble Ticket Management Aggregate Function includes:
-  Resource Trouble Ticket recording
- Identification of the diagnostic actions relevant to identify the resource trouble, depending on the resource type and rules defined by the Resource Problem Strategy and Policy Management function
- Triggering of these diagnostic actions and analysis of their results
- Initialization of the Resource Trouble - or association to an existing Resource Trouble
- Identification of the internal or external actor or team in charge of the Resource
- Trouble Ticket management, follow up and reporting
- Resource Trouble Ticket lifecycle management
- Reporting 
- Escalation procedure.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Analysis -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Trouble Analysis">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.6.2.4</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">253</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Trouble Analysis Resource Trouble Analysis Aggregate Function permits, to identify 
- the trouble causes - that is to say the reasons why the installed resource is in trouble and among these reasons the root cause of the resource trouble 
- and the necessary resolution actions to solve the problem.

It is based on diagnostic actions (Test, inventories and historic consultation, workforce intervention) and rules defined by Resource Problem Strategy and Policy Management. 

The identification of the resource trouble cause can be made through:
- Analysis of associated Service Trouble Ticket Information (first diagnostic)
- Analysis of associated Resource Trouble Ticket Information (first diagnostic)
- Analysis of Resource alarm events (fault of performance data )
- Issuing resource tests, including one time tests or a series of tests over a period of time
- Analysis of previous resource test results, resource usage historic, QoS historic, equipment logs, anomaly historics,  

Remark: If the diagnostic action is a Resource Order, this will be created via the Resource Order Initialization function.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Traffic Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Network Traffic Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.6.3.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">254</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Network Traffic Management The Network Traffic Management AF groups function that identify and solve traffic problems in the service provider's network. These problems could be the result of failures and outages in the network as well as abnormal increases in load.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Usage Management -->

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Usage Management">
<rdf:type rdf:resource = "http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF"/>
<TMF_v1:AFLevel rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1</TMF_v1:AFLevel>
<TMF_v1:hasID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">5.7.1</TMF_v1:hasID>
<TMF_v1:hasUID rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">255</TMF_v1:hasUID>
<rdf:label rdf:datatype = "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#langString">Resource Usage Management The Resource Usage Management aggregate function consist in follow-up of the Resource use, based on Resource. It deals with reports and analysis with an on-the-fly analysis (detailed &amp; aggregated) of the resource uses and comparison with the resource capacity management and with the resource usage analysis forecasts.</rdf:label>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/igork/ontologies/2024/1/TMF_v1#AF.Resource Fraud Detection -->

